ARTICLES = [
{"slug":"uk-gambling-commission-ll-europe-900000-withdrawal-delays-unfair-terms",
"title":"UKGC £900k Settlement Targets Withdrawal Delay Terms",
"category":"Enforcement",
"excerpt":"L & L Europe pays £900,000 over one-at-a-time withdrawal terms, ad hoc payout checks and uneven AML and safer gambling controls.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-23T06:15:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["united-kingdom"],
"related_firms":["harris-hagan","wiggin-llp","mishcon-de-reya-llp"],
"body":[
"On 21 October 2026 the Gambling Commission announced that L & L Europe Limited, which operates seven consumer-facing websites including allbritishcasino.com, casinocasino.com and hypercasino.com, will pay £900,000 under a regulatory settlement. The public statement describes failures across three areas: the handling of customer withdrawals, anti-money laundering controls, and social responsibility. The case is notable less for its size than for its subject. Withdrawal practices are now being treated as a core consumer-protection matter, not a customer-service detail.",
"The withdrawal findings are the most instructive. The operator’s terms allowed only one withdrawal at a time and prevented a customer from submitting a new request until the previous one had been authorised. The Commission considers that term unfair under the Consumer Rights Act 2015. In addition, for some customers every fifth or tenth withdrawal was manually reviewed, and extra information was requested only at that point. The regulator’s position is that where information could reasonably have been requested earlier, holding it back until a payout is pending delays the customer’s own money without a sound reason.",
"The Commission’s Executive Director of Operations framed the expectation plainly: when a customer has winnings, they should be able to withdraw them swiftly. That statement should be read alongside the licence conditions on fair and transparent terms and the requirement that licensees treat customers fairly. Terms that look operationally convenient, such as sequencing withdrawals or batching reviews, will be tested against fairness principles that the consumer-law regime applies directly. Operators should expect the Commission to use consumer-law concepts, including the unfairness test, as a benchmark in licensing and compliance reviews.",
"On anti-money laundering, the findings describe risk controls that relied mainly on financial thresholds and sampling rather than on risk factors or customer risk profiles. The operator also did not always follow its own customer due diligence procedures, including considering the risks of newly opened bank accounts and verifying that funds derived from employment, business income or savings. The recurring theme in UK enforcement is that a policy on paper is not a control in practice. A licensee that departs from its own procedures hands the regulator an easy finding, because the standard it has failed to meet is one it set itself.",
"The social responsibility findings concern consistency. Safer gambling alerts, for matters such as time spent gambling or failed deposits, were active on some of the group’s websites but not across all of them, and the operator had no process for evaluating whether its customer interaction approach worked. Multi-brand groups are particularly exposed here. Where a licence covers several sites, the Commission expects the same protections to apply across the whole estate, and it expects evidence of effectiveness, not just of activity.",
"Practical steps follow directly. Operators should audit withdrawal terms for any provision that restricts the number or sequencing of requests, map exactly when each document or data point can first be requested, and move verification to the earliest reasonable point in the customer journey. They should also test whether AML risk assessments are driven by customer-level risk factors and whether front-line staff follow written procedures in practice. Legal teams should document the reasoning behind any manual review rule, since an unexplained sampling pattern is difficult to defend.",
"The settlement also fits the wider direction of enforcement this year, in which regulatory settlement payments go to the Consolidated Fund and the Commission has signalled it will not hesitate to act where gambling is not fair and open. Operators with UK exposure should treat this case as a checklist for an internal review of payout journeys. UK gambling specialists such as those listed on GamblingLawyers.com can advise on terms review, AML control design and engagement with the Commission before an issue becomes a settlement."
]},
{"slug":"curacao-gaming-authority-licensing-portal-breach-licensee-data-risk-2026",
"title":"Curaçao Portal Breach Raises Licensee Data Questions",
"category":"Regulatory",
"excerpt":"The CGA is retiring its breached licensing portal and launching a temporary replacement; licensees should assess data exposure now.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-23T07:30:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["curacao","netherlands"],
"related_firms":["gaming-legal-group","kalff-katz-and-franssen","cms"],
"body":[
"The Curaçao Gaming Authority has confirmed that its online licensing portal and database, which was managed by an external provider, was subject to unauthorised access between December 2025 and September 2026. The CGA says it discovered on 17 September 2026 that a German digital security researcher had accessed the system and taken the portal offline for an external investigation. On 7 October it announced that it would permanently stop using the existing portal and end its use of the provider’s regulatory services, and a temporary portal was scheduled to launch on 19 October to keep applications and supervision running.",
"The authority has characterised the incident as criminal conduct and a serious matter for itself, the sector and those involved. It reports that it has filed complaints with authorities in Curaçao, Germany and the Netherlands, instructed specialist firms to remove leaked information from the internet, commissioned an independent technical investigation, and is considering civil action. It has also stated that closing the old portal does not invalidate existing licences and that licence holders must continue to meet their obligations. Findings of the independent investigation have not been published.",
"The central open question for licensees is what was exposed. The CGA’s public statements, as reported, do not specify the categories of data, the number of records or whether personal data was involved. A licensing portal typically holds corporate structures, ultimate beneficial owner details, identity documents, source-of-funds material and compliance policies. If any of that was accessed or published, licensees may have their own notification duties, which depend on where the individuals concerned are located and on the data protection regimes that apply to them, including the GDPR for EU-resident individuals.",
"Licensees should therefore take a structured approach. First, inventory what was submitted through the portal since the CGA began its direct-licensing reform, including personal data of directors, shareholders and key persons. Second, ask the CGA in writing what was affected and when, and keep the correspondence. Third, assess whether a data breach notification, to a supervisory authority or to individuals, is required, and document the analysis even if the conclusion is that no notification is needed. Fourth, consider heightened monitoring for impersonation and phishing aimed at named individuals.",
"The incident arrives at a sensitive moment for the Curaçao reform. The CGA has been moving from a master-licence and sub-licence structure to direct supervision of online operators under the LOK, which reaches its second anniversary in December, and it has said it has hired 14 staff since 2024 for licensing, supervision and enforcement. Its reform drivers included agreements with the Netherlands, rising player complaints and a 2024 CFATF assessment. A breach of the central licensing system tests the credibility of precisely the supervisory capacity those reforms were meant to demonstrate.",
"For banks, payment providers and B2B counterparties, the practical consequence is verification. The licence register and the digital seal system were part of what the CGA planned to restore with the temporary portal, and counterparties that rely on a seal as evidence of authorisation should verify licence status against the register itself rather than the displayed badge. Operators, in turn, should expect more frequent due diligence requests and should keep current evidence of their authorisation and supervisory correspondence ready to share.",
"Looking ahead, the CGA says it intends to build and run a new portal itself, with security testing to follow, so the temporary arrangement is a bridge rather than a settled solution. Licensees should plan for resubmission of some information, tolerate interim process changes, and watch for guidance on timelines. Counsel experienced in Curaçao licensing, together with data protection advisers in the jurisdictions where affected individuals sit, can help structure the response and the dialogue with the regulator."
]},
{"slug":"netherlands-ksa-eod-code-37-million-fine-revenue-based-penalty",
"title":"KSA’s €37m Fine Sets a New Illegal Operator Benchmark",
"category":"Enforcement",
"excerpt":"The Dutch regulator’s record €37m fine on EOD Code SRL relies on estimated revenue, with anonymous payments an aggravating factor.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-23T08:45:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["netherlands"],
"related_firms":["kalff-katz-and-franssen","stibbe","akd-benelux-lawyers"],
"body":[
"On 22 October 2026 Dutch media reported that the Kansspelautoriteit (KSA) will fine EOD Code SRL €37 million for offering online casino games to people in the Netherlands without a licence. The amount is reported as the highest the KSA has imposed for illegal operations, surpassing the almost €25 million fine imposed on Novatech in March. The investigation initially focused on Instantcasino, and the operator had previously received a penalty payment order. The reported detail is thin on dates and procedure, and the decision may be subject to objection and appeal.",
"The most significant legal feature is the basis for the amount. As reported, the KSA may set a fine by reference to estimated revenue where the standard penalty would be low compared with what an operator earned from illegal gambling in the Netherlands. The regulator took that approach because it believed EOD Code earned more from Dutch players’ bets than a standard calculation would reflect. For offshore operators, this undermines a familiar planning assumption, namely that the penalty ceiling is a fixed figure that can be priced in as a cost of doing business.",
"The conduct findings illustrate how the regulator builds an enforcement case. KSA employees were able to gamble on the sites with no measures in place to block Dutch users. Several sites lacked visible age verification, which the KSA treated as an aggravating factor. Players could pay with crypto and other anonymous methods, which the regulator said encourages money laundering. The operator’s website also claimed registration and regulation in Costa Rica, a statement that carries no weight as a defence to Dutch licensing requirements and may in fact draw attention to the absence of a Dutch licence.",
"For licensed operators the case is relevant in two ways. First, it supports the channelisation argument that the Dutch regulated market is being protected by increasingly large penalties on unlicensed competitors, which may influence how licence holders approach their own marketing, affiliate and payment relationships. Second, it reinforces the principle that the KSA looks at the whole ecosystem. Affiliates, payment processors and advertisers that touch a prohibited offering can themselves be drawn into scrutiny, and the regulator has been explicit about role-model and influencer liability in recent months.",
"For operators that are not licensed in the Netherlands but have a Dutch customer base, the immediate exposure analysis should be sober. Passive geo-blocking of a domain is not enough if Dutch players can still register, deposit and play, and the regulator has shown it will test this itself. Operators should review IP and payment-based blocking, language and currency targeting, affiliate traffic from Dutch sources, and any marketing that references the Netherlands. They should also consider whether a Dutch licence application is the more defensible long-term strategy.",
"There are open points that practitioners should watch. The amount will be tested if challenged, and an administrative court’s view on revenue-based estimation, on proportionality and on the evidence of Dutch-targeted activity would have wide influence. The reports do not describe any other sanction, but in practice the KSA often combines fines with penalty payments, public naming and requests to payment and hosting providers. Companies that receive an order should obtain Dutch counsel immediately, because objection periods are short.",
"Overall, the decision signals that the Dutch regime is entering a phase in which the cost of unlicensed activity is calibrated to earnings rather than to a statutory cap. Licensed operators, suppliers and payment partners should read it as confirmation that enforcement priorities remain high, and should keep their own compliance evidence in good order. Dutch gambling law specialists in the GamblingLawyers.com directory can advise on enforcement response, licensing strategy and supply-chain risk."
]},
{"slug":"australia-acma-former-afl-players-vegastars-warnings-influencer-liability",
"title":"ACMA Warns Ex-AFL Players Over Offshore Casino Promos",
"category":"Enforcement",
"excerpt":"Formal warnings for two former AFL players show personal exposure for promoting prohibited services, ahead of 2027 athlete-ad limits.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-23T09:20:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["australia"],
"related_firms":["bird-and-bird","cms","pinsent-masons-llp"],
"body":[
"The Australian Communications and Media Authority has issued formal warnings under section 64A of the Interactive Gambling Act to two former AFL players, Mitch Robinson and Rhys Mathieson, for promoting Vegastars, an offshore online casino the regulator had previously classified as a prohibited interactive gambling service. The promotions ran between March and April 2026 on YouTube and Instagram. The warnings were announced on 22 October 2026, days after the ACMA said it would step up enforcement against influencers.",
"The factual findings show how the regulator approaches these cases. The ACMA’s final report on Mr Robinson, dated 30 September 2026, found two instances of prohibited advertising: a podcast episode on 10 March and an Instagram video on 6 April. He had entered two commercial agreements with the operators in March and is reported to have received about AU$120,000. The report on Mr Mathieson, dated 1 October, found three Instagram videos, with documentary evidence of around AU$19,200 in earnings. Both removed the content after receiving notices.",
"The legal point with the widest application is the rejection of ignorance as an answer. Mr Mathieson said he did not know Vegastars was prohibited, and the ACMA responded that ignorance of the law does not exempt individuals from infringement. The warning follows the regulator’s January 2026 formal warning to the operator, Neptune Projects SRL, and its requests to internet service providers to block the main and mirror sites. The sequence tells influencers that checking a service’s status is part of their own responsibility before taking a fee.",
"The statutory exposure is considerable even though these outcomes were warnings. Individuals who promote prohibited gambling services face civil penalties of up to AU$65,520 per contravention, and entities that facilitate access, for example by linking to or directing users to the service, can face penalties of up to AU$2.73 million. Because each post or appearance may be a separate contravention, the aggregate exposure for an active creator can rise quickly. A warning is a lower rung on the enforcement ladder, and a repeat contravention after a warning would be difficult to defend.",
"The timing matters because the regulatory perimeter is about to widen. From 1 January 2027, new wagering advertising restrictions will bar licensed providers from using current or former athletes, social media influencers and other notable people to promote their services. A Senate hearing on 4 August 2026 heard concerns about how the notable-person provision is drafted, with Sportsbet warning it could capture anyone with an Instagram account. Licensed operators therefore face a short window to unwind ambassador and affiliate arrangements and to seek guidance on borderline cases.",
"For operators, agencies and talent managers the compliance response is concrete. Contracts should include warranties and verification mechanics confirming that the advertised service is lawful in Australia, together with termination rights and content takedown timelines. Affiliate and influencer programmes should screen against the ACMA’s list of blocked and notified services. Platforms and intermediaries that host or link to promotions should review their own exposure under the facilitation provisions.",
"The wider lesson is that Australian enforcement is moving down the value chain from offshore operators to the people who give them reach. Licensed wagering providers should expect the ACMA to use the same tools in the notable-person context from 2027. Legal counsel with Australian interactive gambling experience can help review marketing arrangements, advise talent and agencies, and prepare for engagement with the regulator."
]},
{"slug":"india-prog-act-rules-2026-online-money-games-offshore-operators",
"title":"India’s PROG Act Rules: What Operators Must Know",
"category":"Regulatory",
"excerpt":"Online money games are prohibited, social games and esports are conditional, and offshore operators are caught under the 2026 Rules.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-23T10:10:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["india"],
"related_firms":["cms","bird-and-bird","pinsent-masons-llp"],
"body":[
"The Promotion and Regulation of Online Gaming Act, 2025, and the Online Gaming Rules 2026 came into force on 1 May 2026, following the Supreme Court’s ban in August 2025. Together they create a framework that prohibits online money games, permits online social games and registered esports on conditions, and establishes an authority with powers to classify games, direct payment blocking and order website and app blocking. Six months on, the practical questions for operators and investors concern definitions, scope and enforcement priorities.",
"The core prohibition is format-based. An online money game is any game combining a paid entry with a monetary or money-equivalent reward, and neither skill nor chance changes that classification. Offering such a game, or advertising or facilitating payments for it, can be a criminal offence, with penalties of up to three years’ imprisonment, a fine of up to INR 1 crore, or both, and higher penalties for repeat offences. Offshore operators offering such games to users in India are expressly within scope, which removes the argument that a foreign licence or server location takes a service outside the Act.",
"Permitted categories carry conditions. Online social games with no monetary stakes may monetise through subscriptions, one-time access fees, advertising and in-game purchases. Esports must be recognised under the National Sports Governance Act, 2025, involve no betting or staking, and be registered under the 2026 Rules, though participation fees and performance-based prizes are allowed. Cosmetic purchases are generally acceptable where payment has no link to the chance of winning. Virtual tokens that cannot be cashed out may still count as other stakes if they can be used to play other games or to buy items that are available for real money.",
"The areas of uncertainty are where legal advice adds most value. The Act does not define game or gameplay, which leaves adjacent formats such as opinion trading, lotteries, promotional contests and prize quizzes in an unclear position, and these are not automatically unregulated. The definition of online game service provider could be read to reach app stores and aggregators, although the better view is that the obligations fall on the business offering the game. The criteria and process for esports recognition were not prescribed when the Act took effect, and a requirement to notify the authority of any change to payment arrangements could, read literally, capture routine changes such as adding a payment partner.",
"Registration discretion adds a further layer. The government can require registration of notified categories of social games, using factors that include the risk of user harm, participation levels, transaction volumes and the provider’s country of origin. The last factor reduces predictability for foreign publishers. Determinations by the authority are also snapshots, not permanent approvals, and the regulator is expected to look closely at revenue models, money flows and reward redemption, so a product that is compliant at launch can fall out of compliance as its monetisation evolves.",
"Tax interacts with all of this. The Supreme Court upheld 28 per cent GST on the full value of deposits on real-money gaming platforms on 27 May, and a constitutional challenge to the Act remains pending even though the law is in force. Several large operators suspended cash games before the Act took effect and have shifted to free-to-play, advertising and sponsorship-led models. Analysts expect the framework to favour larger, well-capitalised firms and to drive consolidation, which affects both M&A diligence and the structure of any new market entry.",
"For international operators and suppliers, the recommended steps are to map every product against the money-game definition, to review payment flows and any India-facing marketing, and to document the basis on which any social or esports offering is said to fall outside the prohibition. Banks and payment providers should expect blocking directions and should have escalation procedures in place. Given the pending constitutional challenge and the number of undefined terms, counsel should monitor both the authority’s determinations and the court proceedings, and structure contracts with change-in-law protection."
]},
]
